{"kind": "journal", "text": "Approach so far. Batch 1 mapped raw feature families. The panel shows classic cross-sectional structure: medium-to-long horizon REVERSAL (ret 5 to 250 all negative SR, peak at ret(60) SR -0.87), a LOW-VOL premium (std(60), std(120), std(250) negative), a BETTING-AGAINST-BETA premium (beta(60) SR -0.82, beta(250) IC -0.0078, strongest single IC), low correlation to the panel paying off (corrm negative), and range-position measures (maxr, minr) negative, i.e. buy assets near their long-run lows. Only ret(3) is mildly positive, suggesting a very short horizon continuation effect that runs opposite to the longer reversal. Batches 2 to 6 built equal-weight cross-sectional rank blends. Combining sleeves from different families produces very large diversification gains, which implies the sleeve PnLs are close to uncorrelated: neg(minr(250)) alone SR 0.89 and neg(beta(60)) alone SR 0.82 combine to SR 1.55. This is the core structural insight and it is a mechanism, not a curve fit. Batch 5 found that delaying the range sleeve improves it, and batch 6 confirmed it is a broad plateau (delay 3 through 20 all give blend SR 1.58 to 1.74, and standalone neg(delay(minr(250),3)) SR 1.17 beats undelayed 0.89). The economic reading is that the range sleeve is contaminated by the last few days of price action, which carry the opposite, short-horizon continuation sign seen in ret(3); lagging the sleeve strips that contamination out. Selection discipline for the final three. Train SR differences of 0.1 to 0.2 among the top candidates are inside estimation noise, so I will not pick the single highest train SR. I will pick from the centre of parameter plateaus rather than from spikes, prefer high rank IC as the more stable estimator, prefer fewer sleeves over more, and deliberately choose three candidates that differ in their risk sleeve and delay so that the reported set is itself diversified rather than three relabelled copies of one signal.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:12:31.169491+00:00"}
