{"kind": "journal", "text": "Final selection. The winning architecture is a three sleeve equal weight cross sectional rank composite that mixes three effects operating at different horizons and with different signs. Sleeve one, slow reversal: neg(cs_rank(delay(minr(250), k))), buy assets sitting near their 250 day low. Sleeve two, betting against beta: neg(cs_rank(beta(60))). Sleeve three, fast continuation: cs_rank(minr(3)), buy assets sitting high in their 3 day range. The sign flip between the slow and fast range sleeves is the key structural finding and it is corroborated by four independent constructions of the same 3 day effect: ret(3) standalone SR +0.26, neg(ma(3)) triple SR 1.89, maxr(3) triple 1.79 and minr(3) triple 1.98. That convergence is what makes me treat it as a real effect rather than a fitted artefact. Diversification does the heavy lifting: the sleeves score roughly 0.9, 0.8 and 0.5 alone but 1.98 combined, which implies near zero PnL correlation between them. Robustness evidence. The slow range delay is a flat plateau: delays 0, 3, 5, 10, 20 give SR 1.94, 1.98, 1.87, 1.92, 1.86 with IC 0.0132 to 0.0140. Beta lookback 60 is a smooth peak, 40 gives 1.76 and 120 gives 1.63, and beta(60) was the best risk sleeve in every earlier structure too. The fast sleeve decays monotonically in lookback, 3 gives 1.98, 5 gives 1.83, 10 gives 1.54, 15 gives 1.50, an edge peak but with a clean gradient rather than a spike. Things I deliberately rejected. A kurt(20) tilt reached train SR 1.84 but failed its generality test: it added no rank IC standalone, it degraded a different core from 1.23 to 1.19 and it degraded the pure beta sleeve from 0.82 to 0.75, so its apparent benefit was a train specific variance effect. I also rejected volatility scaling by division, ts_z normalization and multiplicative sleeve interactions, all of which underperformed the plain additive rank blend. Reported three. Number one is the plateau best on both SR and IC. Number two is the same architecture at delay 10, mid plateau, included so that the reported set does not stake everything on one delay setting. Number three is a five sleeve version adding low volatility and low panel correlation, lower train SR at 1.84 but joint best IC at 0.0137 and the most diversified across families, included as the structural hedge in case any single sleeve decays out of sample.", "ts": "2026-09-01T07:18:43.890567+00:00"}
{"kind": "report", "top3_chosen": ["add(add(neg(cs_rank(delay(minr(250), 3))), neg(cs_rank(beta(60)))), cs_rank(minr(3)))", "add(add(neg(cs_rank(delay(minr(250), 10))), neg(cs_rank(beta(60)))), cs_rank(minr(3)))", "add(add(add(add(neg(cs_rank(delay(minr(250), 3))), neg(cs_rank(beta(60)))), cs_rank(minr(3))), neg(cs_rank(corrm(60)))), neg(cs_rank(std(250))))"], "chosen_sr_train": [1.9783, 1.9216, 1.8409], "rationale": "All three are equal weight cross sectional rank composites of the same architecture: slow reversal (buy assets near their 250 day low), betting against beta at a 60 day window, and fast 3 day range continuation. The sleeves score about 0.9, 0.8 and 0.5 SR alone but 1.98 combined, so near zero cross sleeve PnL correlation is doing the work rather than any single strong predictor. The slow delay dimension is a flat plateau, SR 1.86 to 1.98 and IC 0.0132 to 0.0140 across delays 0 to 20, and the sign flip between the slow and fast range sleeves is confirmed by four independent constructions of the 3 day continuation effect. I rejected a kurt(20) tilt that reached train SR 1.84 because it carried no standalone rank IC and degraded two other cores, so it was a train specific variance effect. Pick one is the plateau best on both SR and IC. Pick two is the same architecture at a different mid plateau delay so the set does not depend on one delay setting. Pick three adds low volatility and low panel correlation sleeves for a lower train SR but joint best IC and the widest family diversification, as a hedge against any single sleeve decaying.", "n_batches_used": 12, "ts": "2026-09-01T07:18:52.445778+00:00"}
